    content: "# Interview Arena \342\200\224 Complete Full-Stack Application Build Prompt\r\n\r\n## 1. Your Role and Objective\r\n\r\nAct as a senior software architect, product manager, UI/UX designer, full-stack developer, database architect, security engineer, QA engineer, and DevOps engineer.\r\n\r\nBuild a complete, end-to-end web application named **Interview Arena**.\r\n\r\nThis is a startup product where students and job seekers can pay for mock interviews conducted by approved student interviewers or experienced professionals.\r\n\r\nThe application must be a functional, deployable product, not merely a landing page, static dashboard, prototype, or collection of mock screens.\r\n\r\nYou must implement:\r\n\r\n- Frontend and backend.\r\n- Authentication and authorization.\r\n- PostgreSQL database and migrations.\r\n- Candidate dashboard.\r\n- Interviewer dashboard.\r\n- Admin dashboard.\r\n- Interviewer application and approval process.\r\n- Tier A, Tier B, and Tier C interview categories.\r\n- Configurable interview rounds.\r\n- Paid interview booking.\r\n- Real-time public interview queue.\r\n- Atomic interviewer acceptance.\r\n- Payment gateway integration.\r\n- Platform commission and interviewer earnings.\r\n- Refund management.\r\n- Interview scheduling.\r\n- Google Meet integration.\r\n- Notifications and email.\r\n- Interview feedback and ratings.\r\n- Admin financial management.\r\n- Security and audit logging.\r\n- Automated testing.\r\n- Docker and deployment configuration.\r\n- Complete documentation.\r\n\r\n**Important:** Do not stop after creating a plan or designing the frontend. Implement the actual application, connect it to the database, validate the complete workflow, and provide exact instructions for running and deploying it.\r\n\r\nIf the repository already contains code, inspect it first and preserve useful existing functionality.\r\n\r\nWhen credentials are unavailable, implement the integration interface, configuration, setup documentation, and a clearly labeled development fallback. Never pretend that a payment, email, payout, or meeting was successfully completed when it was not.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 2. Product Overview\r\n\r\n## Product name\r\n\r\nInterview Arena\r\n\r\n## Target audience\r\n\r\n- College students.\r\n- Freshers.\r\n- Software engineers.\r\n- Job seekers.\r\n- Candidates preparing for product-based companies.\r\n- Candidates preparing for service-based companies.\r\n- Experienced professionals changing jobs.\r\n- People preparing for technical, behavioral, and system design interviews.\r\n\r\n## Core concept\r\n\r\nThe platform has three interview categories:\r\n\r\n- Tier A: highly competitive company preparation.\r\n- Tier B: established companies and moderately competitive interviews.\r\n- Tier C: entry-level, smaller-company, and fundamentals-focused interviews.\r\n\r\nCandidates select a company preparation track, choose an interview round, pay the applicable price, and join a queue.\r\n\r\nApproved interviewers can see eligible waiting sessions and accept them.\r\n\r\nOnce an interviewer accepts a session, the system must assign that session to exactly one interviewer, notify both participants, and provide a real meeting link through the configured meeting provider.\r\n\r\nAfter the interview, the interviewer submits feedback and earns the configured share of the session fee.\r\n\r\nThe platform retains its configured commission.\r\n\r\nThe application must clearly state that these are simulated practice interviews and that company preparation tracks do not imply official partnerships or guarantee employment.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 3. Technology Stack\r\n\r\nUse a maintainable, production-minded architecture.\r\n\r\nRecommended stack:\r\n\r\n### Frontend\r\n\r\n- Next.js with TypeScript and App Router.\r\n- React.\r\n- Tailwind CSS.\r\n- shadcn/ui or an equivalent accessible component library.\r\n- Responsive desktop, tablet, and mobile layouts.\r\n\r\n### Backend\r\n\r\n- Next.js server actions and route handlers, or a separate Node.js/Express backend if the repository architecture justifies it.\r\n- TypeScript.\r\n- Zod or equivalent input validation.\r\n- REST APIs or a documented, consistent server-action architecture.\r\n\r\n### Database\r\n\r\n- PostgreSQL.\r\n- Prisma ORM or Drizzle ORM.\r\n- Database migrations.\r\n- Foreign keys, indexes, unique constraints, and transactions.\r\n\r\n### Authentication\r\n\r\nUse a maintained authentication library compatible with the selected stack.\r\n\r\nSupport secure email/password authentication, email verification, password reset, and optional OAuth.\r\n\r\n### Payments\r\n\r\nUse Razorpay or another provider that supports the required payment workflow in India.\r\n\r\nUse test mode during development.\r\n\r\n### Meetings\r\n\r\nUse the Google Calendar API to create calendar events with Google Meet conference data.\r\n\r\nNever generate fake Google Meet URLs.\r\n\r\n### Real-time communication\r\n\r\nUse WebSockets, Server-Sent Events, or another appropriate real-time mechanism for queue updates.\r\n\r\nProvide a documented polling fallback if necessary.\r\n\r\n### Email\r\n\r\nUse an adapter for a provider such as Resend, Amazon SES, or Postmark.\r\n\r\n### Testing\r\n\r\nUse appropriate tools for:\r\n\r\n- Unit tests.\r\n- Integration tests.\r\n- End-to-end tests.\r\n\r\n### Deployment\r\n\r\nPrepare the application for deployment using a compatible platform, managed PostgreSQL, environment variables, and CI.\r\n\r\nDocker and Docker Compose should be provided where appropriate.\r\n\r\nUse a modular monolith initially. Do not introduce unnecessary microservices.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 4. User Roles and Account System\r\n\r\nThe application must support three primary roles.\r\n\r\n## A. Candidate\r\n\r\nCandidates can:\r\n\r\n- Register and log in.\r\n- Complete their profile.\r\n- Browse company tracks.\r\n- Select Tier A, B, or C.\r\n- Browse interview rounds.\r\n- Select an interview type.\r\n- Pay for an interview.\r\n- Join a public waiting queue.\r\n- Book a scheduled interview.\r\n- Receive an interviewer assignment.\r\n- Join a meeting.\r\n- View interview history.\r\n- View feedback and scores.\r\n- Rate interviewers.\r\n- Request refunds when eligible.\r\n- Cancel or reschedule sessions according to the configured policy.\r\n- Report problems.\r\n- Manage notifications and account settings.\r\n\r\n## B. Interviewer\r\n\r\nInterviewers can:\r\n\r\n- Apply to become an interviewer.\r\n- Complete the screening process.\r\n- View application status.\r\n- Set their availability.\r\n- View eligible waiting sessions.\r\n- Accept interview requests.\r\n- Conduct interviews.\r\n- Submit structured feedback.\r\n- View assigned and completed sessions.\r\n- View earnings.\r\n- Track pending and available balances.\r\n- View payout history.\r\n- Manage their profile and skills.\r\n- Receive notifications.\r\n- Report technical issues or inappropriate conduct.\r\n\r\nOnly approved interviewers can accept paid sessions.\r\n\r\n## C. Admin\r\n\r\nAdmins can:\r\n\r\n- View platform analytics.\r\n- Manage users.\r\n- Approve or reject interviewer applications.\r\n- Review screening interviews.\r\n- Manage tiers, tracks, rounds, and categories.\r\n- Configure prices and commissions.\r\n- Manage bookings and waiting queues.\r\n- View payment transactions.\r\n- Approve eligible refunds.\r\n- Manage interviewer earnings and payouts.\r\n- Review disputes and user reports.\r\n- Configure refund policies.\r\n- Configure queue timeouts.\r\n- Monitor integration failures.\r\n- View audit logs.\r\n- Manage application settings.\r\n- Monitor database, payment, email, meeting, and background-job health.\r\n\r\nAdmin permissions must be enforced on the server. Hiding admin buttons in the frontend is not sufficient.\r\n\r\n## One account, multiple roles\r\n\r\nA user should be able to use the same account as both a candidate and an interviewer.\r\n\r\nProvide a role switcher.\r\n\r\nMaintain separate candidate and interviewer profile records where appropriate.\r\n\r\nDo not require a person to create two accounts simply to use both features.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 5. Authentication and User Profiles\r\n\r\nImplement:\r\n\r\n- Registration.\r\n- Login.\r\n- Logout.\r\n- Email verification.\r\n- Password reset.\r\n- Session management.\r\n- Optional OAuth.\r\n- Account suspension.\r\n- Account deletion requests.\r\n- Profile updates.\r\n- Terms and privacy acceptance.\r\n\r\nProfile fields may include:\r\n\r\n- Display name.\r\n- Profile image.\r\n- Skills.\r\n- Preferred job roles.\r\n- Experience level.\r\n- Graduation year.\r\n- College or employer, optional.\r\n- Bio.\r\n- Preferred interview languages.\r\n- Timezone.\r\n- Professional links, optional.\r\n\r\nNever store plaintext passwords.\r\n\r\nUse secure password hashing and the authentication framework\'s recommended session management.\r\n\r\nProtect sensitive routes and validate all requests on the server.\r\n\r\nUse rate limiting for login, password reset, payments, queue acceptance, and other sensitive operations.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 6. Tier-Based Interview Categories\r\n\r\nImplement three configurable tiers.\r\n\r\n## Tier A \342\200\224 Top-tier preparation\r\n\r\nInitial price: \342\202\271100 per interview session.\r\n\r\nExample preparation tracks:\r\n\r\n- Google.\r\n- Meta.\r\n- Amazon.\r\n- Microsoft.\r\n- Apple.\r\n- Netflix.\r\n- Adobe.\r\n- Uber.\r\n- Atlassian.\r\n\r\nThese are preparation examples, not official classifications.\r\n\r\n### Typical practice pathway: 3\342\200\2235 rounds\r\n\r\n**Round 1: Online Assessment / Coding Screen**\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\n- Data structures and algorithms.\r\n- Arrays and strings.\r\n- Hashing.\r\n- Binary search.\r\n- Trees and graphs.\r\n- Dynamic programming.\r\n- Time and space complexity.\r\n\r\n**Round 2: Technical Coding Interview**\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\n- Medium and hard coding problems.\r\n- Problem-solving approach.\r\n- Edge cases.\r\n- Complexity analysis.\r\n- Code quality.\r\n- Testing and communication.\r\n\r\n**Round 3: Advanced Technical Interview**\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\n- Advanced problem solving.\r\n- Role-specific technical knowledge.\r\n- Deeper engineering concepts.\r\n- Project-related technical questions.\r\n\r\n**Round 4: System Design**\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\n- High-level architecture.\r\n- API design.\r\n- Database design.\r\n- Caching.\r\n- Load balancing.\r\n- Scalability.\r\n- Reliability.\r\n- Availability.\r\n- Trade-offs.\r\n\r\nMake this round optional for roles where system design is not appropriate.\r\n\r\n**Round 5: Behavioral / Hiring Manager**\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\n- Project discussion.\r\n- Ownership.\r\n- Teamwork.\r\n- Conflict resolution.\r\n- Decision-making.\r\n- Communication.\r\n- Leadership and learning.\r\n\r\nAdmins must be able to configure three, four, or five rounds for a particular track.\r\n\r\n## Tier B \342\200\224 Established company preparation\r\n\r\nInitial price: \342\202\27180 per interview session.\r\n\r\nTypical practice pathway: 2\342\200\2233 rounds.\r\n\r\n**Round 1: Coding and Fundamentals**\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\n- Basic and medium DSA.\r\n- OOP.\r\n- DBMS.\r\n- SQL.\r\n- Operating systems.\r\n- Computer networks.\r\n\r\n**Round 2: Role-Specific Technical Interview**\r\n\r\nExamples:\r\n\r\n- Frontend engineering.\r\n- Backend engineering.\r\n- Full-stack development.\r\n- Data engineering.\r\n- Cloud and DevOps.\r\n- QA and testing.\r\n\r\n**Round 3: Behavioral, Project Discussion, or Basic System Design**\r\n\r\nTopics depend on the selected role.\r\n\r\nAdmins can configure two or three rounds.\r\n\r\n## Tier C \342\200\224 Entry-Level and Fundamentals Preparation\r\n\r\nInitial price: \342\202\27160 per interview session.\r\n\r\nTypical practice pathway: 1\342\200\2232 rounds, with an optional third.\r\n\r\n**Round 1: Fundamentals and Basic Coding**\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\n- Programming fundamentals.\r\n- Basic DSA.\r\n- OOP.\r\n- SQL.\r\n- Problem-solving fundamentals.\r\n\r\n**Round 2: Practical Technical Interview**\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\n- Role-specific questions.\r\n- Small coding exercises.\r\n- Debugging.\r\n- Project discussion.\r\n- Basic technical concepts.\r\n\r\n**Optional Round 3: Behavioral / HR**\r\n\r\nTopics:\r\n\r\n- Introduction.\r\n- Strengths and weaknesses.\r\n- Career goals.\r\n- Communication.\r\n- Basic project discussion.\r\n\r\n## Important round-management requirements\r\n\r\nThe round count must not be hard-coded into the frontend.\r\n\r\nAdmins must be able to configure:\r\n\r\n- Round name.\r\n- Tier.\r\n- Company preparation track.\r\n- Job role.\r\n- Description.\r\n- Topics.\r\n- Difficulty.\r\n- Duration.\r\n- Price.\r\n- Evaluation rubric.\r\n- Prerequisites.\r\n- Display order.\r\n- Active status.\r\n\r\nA candidate must see the recommended round pathway and their progress.\r\n\r\nPayment for one round must not automatically imply payment for all rounds.\r\n\r\nA future bundle feature may be added, but only if it has an explicit price, defined contents, and appropriate payment logic.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 7. Interviewer Registration and Screening\r\n\r\nProvide a separate **Become an Interviewer** experience.\r\n\r\nThe application should explain:\r\n\r\n- Eligibility.\r\n- Responsibilities.\r\n- Expected professionalism.\r\n- Interviewer compensation.\r\n- Platform commission.\r\n- Session cancellation rules.\r\n- Feedback requirements.\r\n- Code of conduct.\r\n\r\nCollect:\r\n\r\n- Skills.\r\n- Experience.\r\n- Preferred roles.\r\n- Languages.\r\n- Preferred tiers.\r\n- Availability.\r\n- Introduction.\r\n- Optional resume or professional profile.\r\n\r\n## Screening process\r\n\r\nEach applicant must complete two screening/practice interviews as required by the initial business model.\r\n\r\nImplement:\r\n\r\n1. Application submission.\r\n2. Screening interview scheduling.\r\n3. Completion of the first screening interview.\r\n4. Completion of the second screening interview.\r\n5. Evaluation against a structured rubric.\r\n6. Admin review.\r\n7. Approval, rejection, or request for additional information.\r\n\r\nPossible application states:\r\n\r\n- Draft.\r\n- Submitted.\r\n- Screening required.\r\n- Under review.\r\n- Approved.\r\n- Rejected.\r\n- Suspended.\r\n\r\nCompleting two interviews must not automatically grant approval.\r\n\r\nAn authorized admin must approve the interviewer.\r\n\r\nOnly approved interviewers can accept paid interview requests.\r\n\r\nStore internal reviewer notes privately.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 8. Interviewer Dashboard\r\n\r\nBuild a complete interviewer dashboard.\r\n\r\nDisplay:\r\n\r\n- Online/offline availability.\r\n- Upcoming interviews.\r\n- Available queue requests.\r\n- Accepted sessions.\r\n- Completed sessions.\r\n- Pending feedback.\r\n- Ratings.\r\n- Completed interview count.\r\n- Pending earnings.\r\n- Available earnings.\r\n- Paid earnings.\r\n- Payout history.\r\n- Application status.\r\n- Notifications.\r\n- Reports and disputes.\r\n\r\n## Queue interface\r\n\r\nEach eligible queue card should display:\r\n\r\n- Interview tier.\r\n- Preparation track.\r\n- Round type.\r\n- Job role.\r\n- Duration.\r\n- Required skills.\r\n- Language.\r\n- Expected compensation.\r\n- Candidate preferences that are safe to disclose.\r\n\r\nDo not expose private candidate information in public queue cards.\r\n\r\nInterviewers can accept or decline eligible requests.\r\n\r\nThe acceptance action must be atomic and server-authorized.\r\n\r\nOnce accepted by one interviewer, the job must disappear from the available queue for everyone else.\r\n\r\nPrevent an interviewer from accepting overlapping sessions or sessions for which they are not qualified.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 9. Candidate Dashboard\r\n\r\nCreate a polished candidate dashboard with:\r\n\r\n- Upcoming interviews.\r\n- Current queue status.\r\n- Approximate waiting time, when meaningful.\r\n- Completed sessions.\r\n- Interview history.\r\n- Total practice sessions.\r\n- Spending summary.\r\n- Feedback and ratings.\r\n- Recommended tracks.\r\n- Recommended next round.\r\n- Profile completion.\r\n- Payment and refund notifications.\r\n\r\nProvide quick actions:\r\n\r\n- Browse interviews.\r\n- Start a practice interview.\r\n- Join the queue.\r\n- Book a scheduled interview.\r\n- View previous feedback.\r\n- Continue the recommended preparation pathway.\r\n\r\n## Interview discovery\r\n\r\nCandidates must be able to filter by:\r\n\r\n- Tier.\r\n- Company preparation track.\r\n- Job role.\r\n- Experience level.\r\n- Round.\r\n- Topic.\r\n- Difficulty.\r\n- Language.\r\n- Duration.\r\n- Price.\r\n- Interviewer rating.\r\n- Immediate queue.\r\n- Scheduled appointment.\r\n\r\nEach interview details page must clearly show the price and refund policy before checkout.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 10. End-to-End Booking Workflow\r\n\r\nImplement this workflow carefully.\r\n\r\n### Step 1: Select an interview\r\n\r\nThe candidate selects:\r\n\r\n- Tier.\r\n- Company preparation track.\r\n- Job role.\r\n- Interview round.\r\n- Preferred language.\r\n- Immediate queue or scheduled session.\r\n\r\n### Step 2: Validate the booking\r\n\r\nThe backend validates:\r\n\r\n- User account status.\r\n- Selected track and round.\r\n- Current server-side price.\r\n- Availability.\r\n- Booking eligibility.\r\n- Duplicate booking requests.\r\n\r\nNever trust a price submitted by the frontend.\r\n\r\n### Step 3: Display checkout\r\n\r\nShow:\r\n\r\n- Session price.\r\n- Applicable taxes, if configured.\r\n- Any separately charged platform fee.\r\n- Total payable amount.\r\n- Cancellation policy.\r\n- Refund policy.\r\n- Expected session duration.\r\n\r\n### Step 4: Payment\r\n\r\nCreate a payment order on the server.\r\n\r\nThe booking must not be marked paid merely because the browser redirects to a success page.\r\n\r\nVerify payment through the provider\'s server-side verification and webhook flow.\r\n\r\n### Step 5: Enter queue\r\n\r\nAfter confirmed payment, create exactly one queue entry.\r\n\r\nDisplay:\r\n\r\n- Queue status.\r\n- Position, if available.\r\n- Approximate waiting time.\r\n- Selected interview.\r\n- Cancellation action.\r\n- Support information.\r\n\r\n### Step 6: Interviewer accepts\r\n\r\nEligible interviewers receive real-time queue updates.\r\n\r\nThe first valid interviewer to successfully claim the booking receives the assignment.\r\n\r\nThe database must guarantee that two interviewers cannot claim the same booking.\r\n\r\n### Step 7: Confirmation\r\n\r\nNotify both participants.\r\n\r\nUpdate the booking state.\r\n\r\nCreate or provision the meeting through the configured provider.\r\n\r\n### Step 8: Interview\r\n\r\nBoth participants join the meeting.\r\n\r\nProvide a session workspace with the interview details, timer, checklist, and feedback form.\r\n\r\n### Step 9: Completion\r\n\r\nThe interviewer submits feedback.\r\n\r\nThe candidate may submit a rating and report a problem.\r\n\r\nUpdate the booking lifecycle and financial ledger according to the configured policy.\r\n\r\n### Step 10: Earnings\r\n\r\nThe interviewer\'s share becomes eligible according to the configured completion and dispute-hold rules.\r\n\r\nIf no interviewer accepts before the timeout, offer continued waiting, rescheduling, or the applicable refund process.\r\n\r\nNever silently retain payment for an unfulfilled session.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 11. Real-Time Queue and Concurrency\r\n\r\nThe queue is a core feature and must be implemented correctly.\r\n\r\nUse WebSockets, SSE, or another suitable real-time technology. A polling fallback is acceptable if documented.\r\n\r\nRequirements:\r\n\r\n- Separate queues by compatible tier, role, track, round, language, and skills.\r\n- Default to FIFO ordering.\r\n- Make any prioritization rules configurable and explainable.\r\n- Show only eligible requests to each interviewer.\r\n- Prevent duplicate queue entries.\r\n- Prevent duplicate payment fulfillment.\r\n- Prevent overlapping interviewer assignments.\r\n- Support cancellation.\r\n- Support interviewer claim expiration.\r\n- Recover abandoned assignments.\r\n- Handle browser refresh and network disconnects.\r\n- Provide a reliable queue state after reconnecting.\r\n- Notify participants when assignment changes.\r\n- Record booking and queue events for auditing.\r\n\r\nUse PostgreSQL transactions, row-level locks, conditional updates, or an equivalent atomic mechanism.\r\n\r\nFor example, if two interviewers accept the same booking simultaneously, only one transaction may succeed. The other must receive a clear message that the session has already been claimed.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 12. Payment Gateway Integration\r\n\r\nIntegrate a real payment provider, preferably Razorpay if the required account capabilities are available.\r\n\r\nUse test mode during development.\r\n\r\n## Required payment flow\r\n\r\n1. Candidate initiates checkout.\r\n2. Backend validates the booking and amount.\r\n3. Backend creates the provider order.\r\n4. Frontend opens the provider checkout.\r\n5. Provider processes the payment.\r\n6. Backend verifies the returned signature where applicable.\r\n7. Backend processes a verified webhook.\r\n8. Payment and booking records are updated idempotently.\r\n9. Exactly one queue entry is created after confirmed payment.\r\n10. Candidate receives a confirmation notification.\r\n\r\n## Security requirements\r\n\r\n- Never expose payment secrets in frontend code.\r\n- Never store card details.\r\n- Verify provider signatures server-side.\r\n- Verify payment amount and currency.\r\n- Match the provider order to the correct user and booking.\r\n- Deduplicate webhook events.\r\n- Handle late and duplicate webhooks.\r\n- Handle failed payments and abandoned checkout.\r\n- Implement retry-safe processing.\r\n- Provide transaction history.\r\n- Provide payment status and receipts.\r\n- Provide refund status.\r\n- Include a local mock provider only for development and automated tests.\r\n\r\nA frontend success redirect must never be the sole proof of payment.\r\n\r\n## Payment states\r\n\r\nSupport appropriate states such as:\r\n\r\n- Pending.\r\n- Authorized.\r\n- Captured or successful.\r\n- Failed.\r\n- Cancelled.\r\n- Partially refunded.\r\n- Refunded.\r\n- Disputed.\r\n\r\nUse the payment provider\'s actual supported states.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 13. Commission and Interviewer Earnings\r\n\r\nThe platform earns a commission from each completed eligible session.\r\n\r\nMake the commission configurable through the admin dashboard.\r\n\r\nFor development, seed an example commission:\r\n\r\n- Platform commission: 20%.\r\n- Interviewer share: 80%.\r\n\r\nThis is a configurable example, not a final business decision.\r\n\r\n## Example\r\n\r\nFor a \342\202\271100 session with a 20% commission on the eligible base amount:\r\n\r\n- Candidate session price: \342\202\271100 before any separately applicable taxes.\r\n- Platform commission: \342\202\27120.\r\n- Interviewer gross share: \342\202\27180.\r\n\r\nDocument how payment-provider fees and applicable taxes are treated. Do not silently deduct unexpected charges.\r\n\r\n## Earnings dashboard\r\n\r\nInterviewers should see:\r\n\r\n- Pending earnings.\r\n- Available earnings.\r\n- Paid earnings.\r\n- Reversed earnings.\r\n- Earnings on hold.\r\n- Session-wise earning details.\r\n- Payout history.\r\n\r\n## Financial ledger\r\n\r\nImplement an immutable or append-only accounting ledger.\r\n\r\nRecord:\r\n\r\n- Successful payment.\r\n- Platform commission.\r\n- Interviewer earning.\r\n- Refund.\r\n- Refund reversal where applicable.\r\n- Payout.\r\n- Payout failure.\r\n- Administrative adjustment.\r\n\r\nNever silently edit historical financial entries. Correct mistakes using explicit reversal or adjustment entries.\r\n\r\n## Payouts\r\n\r\nImplement a payout adapter.\r\n\r\nIf the provider supports marketplace payouts and the business account is eligible, integrate the supported flow.\r\n\r\nOtherwise, provide an admin-managed payout workflow with statuses, references, and reconciliation.\r\n\r\nDo not claim a bank transfer occurred unless the provider or authorized operator confirms it.\r\n\r\nPrevent payout of disputed, reversed, or ineligible earnings.\r\n\r\nBefore production, verify the payment provider\'s support for marketplace payments, split settlements, third-party payouts, KYC, and applicable Indian requirements.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 14. Refund and Cancellation System\r\n\r\nCreate a configurable refund policy.\r\n\r\nHandle:\r\n\r\n- Candidate cancels before interviewer assignment.\r\n- No interviewer accepts before the timeout.\r\n- Interviewer cancels after accepting.\r\n- Candidate does not attend.\r\n- Interviewer does not attend.\r\n- Meeting integration fails.\r\n- Technical problems prevent the session.\r\n- Both parties request rescheduling.\r\n- Duplicate payments.\r\n- Disputed or incomplete interviews.\r\n- Admin-approved goodwill refunds.\r\n\r\nBefore checkout, explain the applicable cancellation and refund policy.\r\n\r\nAdmins must be able to:\r\n\r\n- View refund requests.\r\n- Review the booking and payment.\r\n- Approve or reject eligible requests.\r\n- Initiate provider refunds.\r\n- Track refund status.\r\n- Record reason codes.\r\n- View audit history.\r\n\r\nRefund operations must be permission-controlled and idempotent.\r\n\r\nNever show a refund as completed until the provider confirms it.\r\n\r\nEnsure refunds produce the correct accounting entries.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 15. Scheduling System\r\n\r\nSupport scheduled appointments in addition to immediate queue-based interviews.\r\n\r\nImplement:\r\n\r\n- Interviewer availability.\r\n- Availability calendar.\r\n- Time-off blocks.\r\n- Slot reservation.\r\n- Duration configuration.\r\n- Buffer times.\r\n- Minimum booking notice.\r\n- Maximum daily session limits.\r\n- Rescheduling.\r\n- Cancellation.\r\n- Calendar invitations.\r\n- Reminder notifications.\r\n- Timezone-aware display.\r\n- Conflict prevention.\r\n\r\nStore timestamps consistently in UTC and display times in the user\'s timezone.\r\n\r\nPrevent overlapping bookings through server-side transactional checks.\r\n\r\nAn interviewer must not be assigned two sessions that overlap.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 16. Google Meet Integration\r\n\r\nImplement real Google Meet generation through the Google Calendar API.\r\n\r\nDo not generate fake Meet URLs.\r\n\r\n## Required workflow\r\n\r\n1. Confirm the booking and its eligibility.\r\n2. Check whether a meeting has already been created.\r\n3. Use the configured Google integration to create a calendar event with Meet conference data.\r\n4. Verify that the provider returned a real meeting URL.\r\n5. Store the provider event ID, meeting URL, creation status, and timestamps.\r\n6. Notify the candidate and assigned interviewer.\r\n7. Display the Join Meeting action to authorized participants.\r\n\r\n## Required handling\r\n\r\n- OAuth configuration.\r\n- Appropriate API scopes.\r\n- Token expiry.\r\n- Revoked authorization.\r\n- API quotas.\r\n- Transient failures.\r\n- Duplicate event prevention.\r\n- Safe retries.\r\n- Meeting creation status.\r\n- Meeting URL access control.\r\n- Rescheduling and cancellation behavior.\r\n\r\nDocument the required Google Cloud project setup, API enablement, OAuth consent configuration, redirect URLs, and credentials.\r\n\r\nIf the integration is not configured, clearly display that the meeting provider is unavailable.\r\n\r\nNever show a fake URL as if it were a real meeting.\r\n\r\nPrefer Google Meet or another managed meeting provider over building a custom WebRTC platform for the first release.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 17. Interview Session Workspace\r\n\r\nCreate a dedicated workspace for each interview.\r\n\r\nDisplay:\r\n\r\n- Booking details.\r\n- Candidate and interviewer.\r\n- Tier.\r\n- Company preparation track.\r\n- Round.\r\n- Role.\r\n- Topics.\r\n- Difficulty.\r\n- Duration.\r\n- Scheduled time.\r\n- Meeting status.\r\n- Join Meeting button.\r\n- Interview checklist.\r\n- Timer.\r\n- Notes.\r\n- Feedback form.\r\n- Report problem action.\r\n- No-show controls.\r\n\r\nAllow private notes and clearly define who can access them.\r\n\r\nDo not record audio or video by default.\r\n\r\nIf recording is added later, require informed consent from both participants and implement secure storage, access control, retention, and deletion policies.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 18. Interview Feedback and Ratings\r\n\r\nAfter the interview, require the interviewer to submit structured feedback.\r\n\r\nPossible rubric criteria:\r\n\r\n- Problem understanding.\r\n- Clarifying questions.\r\n- Problem-solving approach.\r\n- Technical correctness.\r\n- Edge cases.\r\n- Code quality.\r\n- Testing.\r\n- Time and space complexity.\r\n- Domain knowledge.\r\n- Communication.\r\n- Collaboration.\r\n- Time management.\r\n\r\nThe rubric must adapt to the interview type.\r\n\r\nProvide:\r\n\r\n- Criterion-level ratings.\r\n- Written feedback.\r\n- Strengths.\r\n- Areas for improvement.\r\n- Recommended topics.\r\n- Recommended next round.\r\n- Overall practice readiness.\r\n\r\nCandidates can rate the interview experience and report concerns.\r\n\r\nPrevent duplicate reviews for the same session.\r\n\r\nProvide moderation tools for inappropriate reviews and reports.\r\n\r\nMake it clear that practice scores are not official employer hiring decisions.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 19. Notifications and Email\r\n\r\nImplement an in-app notification center and an email-provider adapter.\r\n\r\nSend notifications for:\r\n\r\n- Registration.\r\n- Email verification.\r\n- Password reset.\r\n- Interviewer application submission.\r\n- Interviewer approval or rejection.\r\n- Payment success or failure.\r\n- Queue entry.\r\n- Interviewer assignment.\r\n- Queue timeout.\r\n- Booking confirmation.\r\n- Rescheduling.\r\n- Cancellation.\r\n- Meeting link availability.\r\n- Upcoming interview reminders.\r\n- Interview completion.\r\n- Feedback availability.\r\n- Refund status.\r\n- Reports and disputes.\r\n- Payout status.\r\n- Admin announcements.\r\n\r\nUse configurable reminder timings, such as 24 hours and 15 minutes before a scheduled interview.\r\n\r\nImplement retry logic, delivery status, and deduplication.\r\n\r\nNever log password-reset tokens or secret credentials.\r\n\r\nIf email credentials are missing, clearly identify email delivery as unconfigured and provide setup instructions.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 20. Admin Dashboard \342\200\224 Mandatory\r\n\r\nBuild a full admin dashboard, not just a visual mockup.\r\n\r\n## Admin overview\r\n\r\nShow real database-backed metrics:\r\n\r\n- Total users.\r\n- Active users.\r\n- Candidates.\r\n- Interviewer applicants.\r\n- Approved interviewers.\r\n- Sessions completed.\r\n- Sessions by tier.\r\n- Sessions by track and round.\r\n- Successful payments.\r\n- Gross payment volume.\r\n- Refund totals.\r\n- Platform commission revenue.\r\n- Interviewer earnings liabilities.\r\n- Payout totals.\r\n- Queue waiting times.\r\n- Acceptance rate.\r\n- Timeout rate.\r\n- No-show rate.\r\n- Disputes.\r\n- Failed webhooks and background jobs.\r\n- Integration health.\r\n\r\nProvide date filters and clear definitions for financial metrics.\r\n\r\nNever invent live analytics.\r\n\r\n## User management\r\n\r\nAdmins can:\r\n\r\n- Search users.\r\n- Filter by role and status.\r\n- View authorized profile details.\r\n- Review interview history.\r\n- Approve or suspend accounts.\r\n- Manage roles.\r\n- Ban or reactivate users.\r\n- Record reasons for consequential actions.\r\n\r\nEvery sensitive action must be audited.\r\n\r\n## Interviewer management\r\n\r\nAdmins can:\r\n\r\n- View applications.\r\n- Review screening interviews.\r\n- Evaluate screening rubrics.\r\n- Approve or reject applications.\r\n- Request additional information.\r\n- Configure interviewer qualifications.\r\n- View quality metrics.\r\n- Review reports and disputes.\r\n- Suspend interviewers when appropriate.\r\n\r\n## Catalog management\r\n\r\nAdmins can:\r\n\r\n- Create and edit tiers.\r\n- Configure prices.\r\n- Create company preparation tracks.\r\n- Configure job roles.\r\n- Configure skills and topics.\r\n- Create and edit rounds.\r\n- Set durations.\r\n- Set difficulty.\r\n- Configure rubrics.\r\n- Define prerequisites.\r\n- Enable and disable catalog items.\r\n\r\nArchive historical catalog records rather than deleting records required by existing bookings.\r\n\r\n## Booking and queue management\r\n\r\nAdmins can:\r\n\r\n- Search all bookings.\r\n- Inspect booking events.\r\n- Find stuck queue entries.\r\n- Find expired interviewer claims.\r\n- Identify paid sessions without an interviewer.\r\n- Identify meeting-generation failures.\r\n- Reassign eligible sessions.\r\n- Cancel or reschedule sessions.\r\n- Escalate unresolved cases.\r\n- Record reasons for manual overrides.\r\n\r\n## Financial management\r\n\r\nAdmins can:\r\n\r\n- View payment transactions.\r\n- View webhook events.\r\n- Review refund requests.\r\n- Initiate authorized refunds.\r\n- Inspect financial ledgers.\r\n- View interviewer earnings.\r\n- Manage payout batches.\r\n- View failed payouts.\r\n- Reconcile payment totals.\r\n- Export authorized reports.\r\n\r\nProtect financial exports and audit access.\r\n\r\n## Settings\r\n\r\nProvide configurable settings for:\r\n\r\n- Tier prices.\r\n- Commission percentage.\r\n- Round definitions.\r\n- Queue timeout.\r\n- Claim timeout.\r\n- Reminder timings.\r\n- Cancellation rules.\r\n- Refund rules.\r\n- Earnings hold period.\r\n- Payout settings.\r\n- Feature flags.\r\n- Maintenance mode.\r\n- Email integration.\r\n- Meeting integration.\r\n- Payment integration.\r\n\r\nNever expose provider secrets in the admin interface.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 21. Database Schema\r\n\r\nUse PostgreSQL and create actual migrations.\r\n\r\nThe following entities are required conceptually. You may adapt the names to the selected ORM.\r\n\r\n## Identity\r\n\r\n- `users`\r\n- `user_roles`\r\n- `candidate_profiles`\r\n- `interviewer_profiles`\r\n- `interviewer_applications`\r\n- `interviewer_screenings`\r\n- Authentication sessions as required.\r\n- Password-reset token records if required.\r\n\r\n## Catalog\r\n\r\n- `tiers`\r\n- `company_tracks`\r\n- `job_roles`\r\n- `skills`\r\n- `track_skills`\r\n- `languages`\r\n- `round_templates`\r\n\r\n## Scheduling and interviews\r\n\r\n- `interviewer_availability`\r\n- Availability exceptions or time-off blocks.\r\n- `bookings`\r\n- `queue_entries`\r\n- `booking_events`\r\n- `meeting_sessions`\r\n- `interview_notes`\r\n- `interview_feedback`\r\n- `reviews`\r\n\r\n## Payments and accounting\r\n\r\n- `payment_orders`\r\n- `payment_transactions`\r\n- `webhook_events`\r\n- `refunds`\r\n- `ledger_accounts` or equivalent accounting structures.\r\n- `ledger_entries`\r\n- `payouts`\r\n- Receipts or invoice records where required.\r\n\r\n## Operations\r\n\r\n- `notifications`\r\n- `user_reports`\r\n- `disputes`\r\n- `admin_audit_logs`\r\n- `app_settings`\r\n- Feature flags.\r\n- Idempotency records where needed.\r\n\r\n## Database requirements\r\n\r\n- Proper foreign keys.\r\n- Appropriate indexes.\r\n- Unique normalized email addresses.\r\n- Unique provider event IDs.\r\n- Unique idempotency keys.\r\n- Transactional booking assignment.\r\n- Transactional payment fulfillment.\r\n- Transactional refund and ledger operations.\r\n- Transactional payout accounting.\r\n- Append-only financial history.\r\n- Safe archival and deletion behavior.\r\n- Migration and seed commands.\r\n- Fake development seed users and catalog data.\r\n\r\nDo not seed production credentials or real personal information.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 22. API and Backend Architecture\r\n\r\nImplement validated and authorized APIs or server actions for:\r\n\r\n- Authentication.\r\n- User profiles.\r\n- Candidate dashboard.\r\n- Interviewer dashboard.\r\n- Admin dashboard.\r\n- Catalog search.\r\n- Interviewer applications.\r\n- Screening and approvals.\r\n- Availability management.\r\n- Booking creation.\r\n- Checkout and payment order creation.\r\n- Payment verification.\r\n- Webhook processing.\r\n- Queue join and cancellation.\r\n- Queue status.\r\n- Atomic interviewer acceptance.\r\n- Scheduling and rescheduling.\r\n- Meeting creation and retries.\r\n- Interview start and completion.\r\n- Feedback submission.\r\n- Reviews.\r\n- Reports and disputes.\r\n- Refunds.\r\n- Earnings.\r\n- Payouts.\r\n- Notifications.\r\n- Admin configuration and audit logs.\r\n\r\nValidate all inputs.\r\n\r\nEnforce authorization on the server.\r\n\r\nUse consistent error responses.\r\n\r\nUse rate limiting for sensitive endpoints.\r\n\r\nNever trust user-supplied prices, roles, commission amounts, payment states, or interviewer approval status.\r\n\r\nKeep payment, booking, and queue states consistent.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 23. Booking and Payment State Machines\r\n\r\nUse explicit state transitions.\r\n\r\nExample booking states:\r\n\r\n- `draft`\r\n- `awaiting_payment`\r\n- `payment_pending`\r\n- `paid`\r\n- `queued`\r\n- `claimed`\r\n- `scheduled`\r\n- `meeting_pending`\r\n- `ready`\r\n- `in_progress`\r\n- `completed`\r\n- `cancelled`\r\n- `no_show_candidate`\r\n- `no_show_interviewer`\r\n- `disputed`\r\n- `refund_pending`\r\n- `refunded`\r\n- `failed`\r\n\r\nKeep payment status separate from booking status if appropriate.\r\n\r\nFor every transition, define:\r\n\r\n- Authorized actor.\r\n- Preconditions.\r\n- Database transaction.\r\n- Notifications.\r\n- Financial consequences.\r\n- Audit event.\r\n- Retry behavior.\r\n- Recovery procedure.\r\n\r\nUse background jobs for:\r\n\r\n- Queue timeouts.\r\n- Expired claims.\r\n- Booking reminders.\r\n- Webhook retries.\r\n- Meeting creation retries.\r\n- Stuck queue recovery.\r\n- Payout processing.\r\n\r\nEvery background job must be safe to retry and idempotent.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 24. UI/UX Requirements\r\n\r\nCreate a polished, modern SaaS product.\r\n\r\nDo not generate a generic template with disconnected pages.\r\n\r\nUse:\r\n\r\n- Consistent typography.\r\n- Clear navigation.\r\n- Professional color palette.\r\n- Responsive layouts.\r\n- Accessible components.\r\n- Loading states.\r\n- Skeleton screens.\r\n- Error states.\r\n- Empty states.\r\n- Confirmation dialogs.\r\n- Inline validation.\r\n- Toast notifications.\r\n- Search, sorting, filters, and pagination.\r\n- Keyboard accessibility.\r\n- Useful mobile layouts.\r\n- Light/dark mode if practical.\r\n\r\nNo dead buttons.\r\n\r\nNo fake testimonials.\r\n\r\nNo fake payment success.\r\n\r\nNo fake ratings or analytics presented as live data.\r\n\r\n## Required screens\r\n\r\n1. Landing page.\r\n2. Registration and login.\r\n3. Email verification.\r\n4. Password reset.\r\n5. Candidate onboarding.\r\n6. Candidate dashboard.\r\n7. Interview catalog.\r\n8. Track details.\r\n9. Checkout.\r\n10. Payment status.\r\n11. Real-time queue.\r\n12. Booking details.\r\n13. Meeting workspace.\r\n14. Interview history.\r\n15. Feedback/results.\r\n16. Become-an-interviewer page.\r\n17. Interviewer application.\r\n18. Screening status.\r\n19. Interviewer dashboard.\r\n20. Availability calendar.\r\n21. Interviewer queue.\r\n22. Interview session workspace.\r\n23. Interviewer earnings.\r\n24. Payout history.\r\n25. Notification center.\r\n26. Account settings.\r\n27. Admin overview.\r\n28. Admin user management.\r\n29. Admin interviewer applications.\r\n30. Admin tier and catalog management.\r\n31. Admin round management.\r\n32. Admin booking and queue management.\r\n33. Admin payment and refund management.\r\n34. Admin ledger and payout management.\r\n35. Admin disputes and reports.\r\n36. Admin integration health.\r\n37. Admin settings.\r\n38. Audit log.\r\n39. Terms and privacy.\r\n40. Refund/cancellation policy.\r\n41. FAQ and support.\r\n42. Error and not-found pages.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 25. Security and Privacy\r\n\r\nFollow OWASP-aligned practices.\r\n\r\nImplement:\r\n\r\n- Secure password hashing.\r\n- Secure sessions and cookies.\r\n- HTTPS in production.\r\n- CSRF protection where applicable.\r\n- XSS prevention.\r\n- Parameterized database queries.\r\n- Server-side authorization.\r\n- Object-level access control.\r\n- Input validation.\r\n- Rate limiting.\r\n- Webhook signature verification.\r\n- Replay protection.\r\n- Idempotency.\r\n- Safe error messages.\r\n- Security headers.\r\n- Least-privilege credentials.\r\n- Audit logs.\r\n- Backup and restore documentation.\r\n- Data-retention rules.\r\n- Account deletion/export workflows where appropriate.\r\n\r\nNever commit credentials to source control.\r\n\r\nNever expose server secrets in frontend bundles.\r\n\r\nNever expose private meeting URLs or financial records to unauthorized users.\r\n\r\nDo not store audio/video recordings by default.\r\n\r\nInclude a production-launch checklist for privacy, tax, payment-provider terms, marketplace payouts, and applicable Indian legal requirements. Do not claim that the application is legally compliant without professional review.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 26. Environment Variables and Credentials\r\n\r\nCreate `.env.example` containing placeholders and comments.\r\n\r\nInclude the variables required for:\r\n\r\n- Application URL.\r\n- Runtime environment.\r\n- PostgreSQL connection.\r\n- Authentication secret.\r\n- OAuth configuration, if enabled.\r\n- Payment provider public key or merchant identifier.\r\n- Payment provider server secret.\r\n- Payment webhook secret.\r\n- Google OAuth client ID.\r\n- Google OAuth client secret.\r\n- Google redirect URI.\r\n- Email provider API key.\r\n- Email sender address.\r\n- Optional real-time service configuration.\r\n- Optional monitoring and error reporting.\r\n\r\nUse the actual variable names selected by the implementation.\r\n\r\nNever hard-code credentials.\r\n\r\nNever commit `.env`.\r\n\r\nDo not ask the user to paste production secrets into source code or a public prompt.\r\n\r\nValidate required configuration and show clear errors without exposing secret values.\r\n\r\nUse separate credentials for development, staging, and production.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n# 27. Testing Requirements\r\n\r\nCreate meaningful automated tests.\r\n\r\n## Unit tests\r\n\r\nTest:\r\n\r\n- Tier pricing.\r\n- Commission calculations.\r\n- Refund calculations.\r\n- Ledger entries.\r\n- Booking state transitions.\r\n- Queue eligibility.\r\n- Interviewer matching.\r\n- Claim expiration.\r\n- Permissions.\r\n- Configuration validation.\r\n\r\n## Integration tests\r\n\r\nTest:\r\n\r\n- Registration and authentication.\r\n- Role-based authorization.\r\n- Booking creation.\r\n- Payment webhook success.\r\n- Payment failure.\r\n- Invalid webhook signature.\r\n- Duplicate webhook delivery.\r\n- Exactly one queue entry after confirmed payment.\r\n- Concurrent interviewer acceptance.\r\n- Rejection of overlapping sessions.\r\n- Queue timeout recovery.\r\n- Refund and ledger consistency.\r"
