    thinking: "**Defining User Roles & Accounts**\n\nI\'m zeroing in on the Interview Arena\'s user roles and account system. The concept is clear: a unified account handling multiple roles (Candidate, Interviewer, Admin) with role switching. I need to focus on authentication (email/password, sessions, profile completion) and then, I\'ll dive into the Candidate profile details, like target roles, experience level, and graduation year.\n\n\n**Expanding Profile Definitions**\n\nI\'m now detailing the user profiles. My focus is on fleshing out Candidate profiles with specifics like college/employer, bio, languages, and timezone. Furthermore, I\'ve got a handle on the Interviewer profile and application flow, including screening statuses, the two-interview rubric, and the required admin approvals, alongside online/offline toggling. I\'m also specifying capabilities and configurations.\n\n\n**Designing Interview Tiers and Booking**\n\nI\'m presently working out the Interview Arena\'s tiered pricing model (Tier B, Tier C) and the associated interview round structures for each, considering parameters like difficulty, topics covered, and rubrics. Moreover, I\'m defining a step-by-step booking system, moving from tier selection through company track and role, to mode (immediate queue or scheduled). Finally, I will specify payment and queue entry.\n\n\n**Analyzing Financial Processes**\n\nI\'m now detailing payments, commissions, and the financial ledger. I\'ve designed a system for payment order creation, verification using signature verification simulation, and a webhook handler. I\'m focusing on an immutable financial ledger to record all transactions (payments, commissions, earnings, refunds, etc.). The commission rate will be configurable in Admin settings, alongside a breakdown of interviewer earnings (pending, available, paid). I\'ve planned out the refund workflows (candidate cancel, timeout, no-show, etc.) with server-side approvals and ledger updates, as well as test modes. Next, I\'ll specify a robust scheduling system.\n\n\n**Defining Scheduling Features**\n\nI\'m now detailing the system for booking specific slots and ensuring conflict prevention in scheduled interviews. I\'m also planning the Google Meet integration, including real architecture using the Google Calendar API conference data handler and clear status indicators if credentials are not provided. There will be a documented fallback. Finally, I\'m working on the session workspace and the post-interview rubrics, including live participant info and a structured feedback form for each participant.\n\n\n**Detailing Review and Architecture**\n\nI\'m now detailing the candidate-interviewer rating system, dispute mechanisms, and practice readiness recommendations. I\'m also architecting the backend: Express on `server.ts` handles API endpoints under `/api/*` with Vite middlewares in dev, and SSE for real-time queue updates. The database schema in `migrations/0001_initial_schema.sql` is defined, with a TypeScript schema in `src/db/schema.ts`. Full documentation and a comprehensive test suite are in progress.\n\n\n**Verifying Server Configuration**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a robust `server.ts` file. My goal is a clean setup that handles API routes, dynamically utilizes Vite\'s development server, and serves static files in production. The key elements are the `express` app, API routes mounted at `/api/...`, and integration with Vite\'s middleware in development. For production, the plan is to serve the `dist` static files and have a fallback for `index.html`. Finally, I\'ll ensure the server listens on the correct port and host. I am building the server setup using Vite 5/6/8.\n\n\n**Refining Backend Structure**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the `package.json` scripts and test configurations. I\'ve confirmed that the built-in Node.js test runner provides a fast and comprehensive testing framework with `tsx tests/run-all.ts`. Moreover, I\'m identifying the core entities and data structures, including users, and candidate profiles, to inform the PostgreSQL schema.\n\n\n**Specifying Profile Data**\n\nI\'m now detailing the database schema, concentrating on `candidate_profiles` and `interviewer_profiles`. I\'ve outlined fields like `user_id`, `target_roles`, `experience_level`, and `graduation_year` for candidates. Interviewers\' data includes `headline`, `bio`, `experience_years`, `skills`, and status, incorporating applications, tiers, tracks, and rounds.\n\n\n**Defining Booking Flow Details**\n\nI\'m now detailing the database schema with a focus on booking-related tables and their attributes. I\'ve designed `bookings` to encompass all interview details, including statuses, scheduling information, and financial data. I\'ve also incorporated `queue_entries`, interviewer availability, and payment orders. I\'m ensuring data consistency and completeness for the booking process.\n\n\n**Elaborating Financial Tables**\n\nI\'m now expanding the database schema, focusing on intricate financial aspects. I\'m adding `refunds`, detailing refund reference, payment order, booking ID, and status. I am also implementing `ledger_entries`, which include booking IDs, user IDs, transaction types, amounts, and descriptions. Moreover, I\'m specifying tables for `interviewer_earnings` and `payouts`, with details on status, method, and amounts.\n\n\n**Structuring Data Management**\n\nI\'m now focusing on a modular file structure to manage the application effectively. The plan is to create `server.ts` to handle Express and Vite integration. I\'m also preparing `src/server/store.ts` for in-memory transactional data, complete with demo data. ACID transaction simulation with row-level mutex locks will be provided.\n\n\n**Organizing Route Structures**\n\nI\'m now architecting the backend routes for Interview Arena. I\'m structuring them logically, which includes `auth`, `catalog`, `bookings`, and `queue`, alongside `payments` and `interviewer`, and `feedback` routes. I\'ve also implemented `admin` routes and `notifications` now, all of which are defined and implemented.\n\n\n**Finalizing System Documentation**\n\nI\'m now putting the finishing touches on documentation and testing. I\'m verifying the full PostgreSQL DDL schema in `migrations/0001_initial_schema.sql` and the associated seed scripts. I\'ve also detailed the `Dockerfile`, `docker-compose.yml`, and comprehensive tests in `tests/run-all.ts`. Finally, I\'m completing all frontend components.\n\n\n**Finalizing Component Design**\n\nI\'m now detailing the system\'s frontend components. I\'ve designed a transparent pricing breakdown, cancellation and refund policy, and a simulated Razorpay gateway modal with test modes. Also I\'ve detailed the `QueueTrackerModal`, `InterviewerDashboard`, `InterviewerApplication`, and `SessionWorkspace` components, each providing key features and information for users.\n\n\n**Designing Feedback Systems**\n\nI\'m now detailing the system\'s feedback components. I\'ve built the `FeedbackModal.tsx` for multi-criteria rubrics, and the `ReviewModal.tsx` for candidate ratings. Additionally, the `AdminDashboard.tsx` is being built out, now including platform analytics, user management, and catalog/tier updates.\n\n\n\n"
