  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Examining UI Components**\n\nI\'m now diving into the UI components: `QueueTrackerModal.tsx`, `FeedbackModal.tsx`, `ReviewModal.tsx`, `InterviewerDashboard.tsx`, `InterviewerApplication.tsx`, `PolicyModals.tsx`, and `Footer.tsx`. My focus is to understand how each one utilizes and interacts with the theme.\n\n\n**Analyzing Theme Conflicts**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the root causes of the light theme issues. The `src/index.css` file contained conflicting styles: the `html.light .text-white` override broke button colors, and opacity classes weren\'t properly mapped, leading to inconsistent backgrounds. Pastel badges became invisible on light backgrounds. Also, the logo, header gradients, and welcome banners were unreadable in light mode due to poor color contrast.\n\n\n**Refactoring Authentication Logic**\n\nI\'m removing the Google Client ID requirement to streamline the setup process. I\'ve updated `.env.example`, and made corresponding changes to `src/types/index.ts` to adjust the `AppSettings` interface. Furthermore, I replaced `googleMeetConfigured` with `inHouseMeetingEnabled` in the `src/server/store.ts` file, and adjusted the meeting provider and URL settings within `claimQueueEntryAtomic` and `seedInitialBookings`.\n\n\n**Implementing In-House Meetings**\n\nI\'ve standardized the `meetingProvider` to `\'IN_HOUSE_MEET\'` across bookings and routes, specifically within `src/server/routes/bookings.ts`. I\'m updating the UI components, such as `CandidateDashboard.tsx` and `AdminDashboard.tsx`, to accurately reflect the new meeting type as \"In-House WebRTC Meeting\" and \"In-House Video Meeting Room,\" respectively. Furthermore, I will create a new badge to represent the update.\n\n**Refining Theme Consistency**\n\nI\'ve resolved the conflicting style issues in `src/index.css` to fix the light theme problems. I\'ve removed the `html.light .text-white` rule, which was overwriting button colors. I\'m introducing explicit rules for the light theme to ensure consistent text colors and background opacities. I\'m focusing on ensuring all UI elements like headings, text, and backgrounds have sufficient contrast in light mode, as well as updating the other UI components to match.\n\n\n**Adjusting Light Mode Styles**\n\nI\'m making further refinements to light mode. I\'m focusing on text and background colors to ensure strong contrast. Specifically, I\'m updating surfaces to be white or light gray with soft borders and shadows. I\'m also carefully handling button text colors to maintain readability. I\'m modifying modal backdrops and colored badge styles to achieve a consistent aesthetic.\n\n\n**Defining Light Mode Elements**\n\nI\'m now nailing down the specific light mode styles for crucial components. I\'m setting a vibrant amber color for the background with darker text for emphasis. The rose badges will have a soft, complementary background with rose-colored text, and the slate badges will use a light background with dark text. For interactive elements, I\'m setting white backgrounds, grey borders, and dark text. Finally, for the video meeting workspace, I\'ll retain a dark canvas for the video feeds while the surrounding panels adopt the light theme.\n\nI\'ve also adjusted the `Navbar.tsx` component. In light mode, the brand name will use a dark slate color, the header has a clean white background with a light border. Active navigation links will have an indigo background and dark indigo text.\n\n\n"
