  planner_response {
    response: "### 1. Why Was It Asking for Google Client ID?\n\nThe AI Studio interface was prompting you for `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` and `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` because `.env.example` contained leftover environment variable placeholders from earlier Google Meet integration experiments.\n\n**Fix Applied:**\n* **Removed all Google OAuth variables** (`GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, `GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI`) from `.env.example` and the configuration store.\n* The meeting solution is **100% in-house** via embedded WebRTC with local camera/mic streaming, screen sharing, live chat, and a code runner. It requires **no Google credentials, no Google Cloud project, and no external OAuth setup**. AI Studio will no longer request any Google Client IDs.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Light / White Theme Styling & Color Issues Fixed\n\nThe color inconsistencies in the light theme have been resolved:\n\n1. **Button & Action Contrast**:\n   * Removed a blanket CSS override (`.text-white { color: #0f172a !important; }`) that previously forced text on primary buttons (e.g., Indigo, Emerald, Rose buttons) to dark slate, which had created dark text on dark backgrounds.\n   * Buttons and colored action elements now retain crisp, high-contrast white text (`#ffffff`).\n\n2. **Colorful Badges & Status Pills**:\n   * Previously, light-tinted labels (like `text-indigo-300`, `text-emerald-300`, `text-amber-300`) were washed out against white cards.\n   * They now use rich, saturated tones (`#3730a3`, `#065f46`, `#92400e`, `#9f1239`) paired with soft pastel backgrounds (`#e0e7ff`, `#d1fae5`, `#fef3c7`), providing high contrast and clear readability.\n\n3. **Brand Logo & Header**:\n   * The \"Interview Arena\" header logo formerly used a gradient that faded into white, making it invisible on white backgrounds. It now renders in sharp slate (`#0f172a`) in light mode and gradient in dark mode.\n   * Navigation links now use subtle indigo highlights (`bg-indigo-50 text-indigo-700`) instead of dark boxes on the white navigation bar.\n\n4. **Candidate Welcome Banner**:\n   * Replaced the dark navy background with an indigo-to-amber soft gradient card in light mode (`bg-gradient-to-r from-indigo-50 via-white to-amber-50`) with clear typography and styled action chips.\n\n5. **In-House Meeting Room**:\n   * The video feed stage retains a dark theater canvas (`#0a0f1d`) so video feeds and aspect ratios stand out, while the meeting controls, chat sidebar, and code editor adapt cleanly to whichever theme (Light or Dark) is active."
    thinking: "**Confirming Server Health**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the development server is up and responding with a 200 OK!\n\nI\'ve also summarized why AI Studio was asking for Google Client ID and Secrets. The `.env.example` file initially contained placeholders for Google credentials, and I\'ve detailed this for clarity.\n\n\n**Eliminating Google Dependency**\n\nI\'ve completely removed Google OAuth and Meet dependencies! The AI Studio now uses a native WebRTC solution and doesn\'t require any Google credentials. I deleted the now-unnecessary `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, and `GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI` from the `.env.example` file to reflect this.\n\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "### 1. Why Was It Asking for Google Client ID?\n\nThe AI Studio interface was prompting you for `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID` and `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` because `.env.example` contained leftover environment variable placeholders from earlier Google Meet integration experiments.\n\n**Fix Applied:**\n* **Removed all Google OAuth variables** (`GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, `GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI`) from `.env.example` and the configuration store.\n* The meeting solution is **100% in-house** via embedded WebRTC with local camera/mic streaming, screen sharing, live chat, and a code runner. It requires **no Google credentials, no Google Cloud project, and no external OAuth setup**. AI Studio will no longer request any Google Client IDs.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Light / White Theme Styling & Color Issues Fixed\n\nThe color inconsistencies in the light theme have been resolved:\n\n1. **Button & Action Contrast**:\n   * Removed a blanket CSS override (`.text-white { color: #0f172a !important; }`) that previously forced text on primary buttons (e.g., Indigo, Emerald, Rose buttons) to dark slate, which had created dark text on dark backgrounds.\n   * Buttons and colored action elements now retain crisp, high-contrast white text (`#ffffff`).\n\n2. **Colorful Badges & Status Pills**:\n   * Previously, light-tinted labels (like `text-indigo-300`, `text-emerald-300`, `text-amber-300`) were washed out against white cards.\n   * They now use rich, saturated tones (`#3730a3`, `#065f46`, `#92400e`, `#9f1239`) paired with soft pastel backgrounds (`#e0e7ff`, `#d1fae5`, `#fef3c7`), providing high contrast and clear readability.\n\n3. **Brand Logo & Header**:\n   * The \"Interview Arena\" header logo formerly used a gradient that faded into white, making it invisible on white backgrounds. It now renders in sharp slate (`#0f172a`) in light mode and gradient in dark mode.\n   * Navigation links now use subtle indigo highlights (`bg-indigo-50 text-indigo-700`) instead of dark boxes on the white navigation bar.\n\n4. **Candidate Welcome Banner**:\n   * Replaced the dark navy background with an indigo-to-amber soft gradient card in light mode (`bg-gradient-to-r from-indigo-50 via-white to-amber-50`) with clear typography and styled action chips.\n\n5. **In-House Meeting Room**:\n   * The video feed stage retains a dark theater canvas (`#0a0f1d`) so video feeds and aspect ratios stand out, while the meeting controls, chat sidebar, and code editor adapt cleanly to whichever theme (Light or Dark) is active."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 2
